{"name":"Stamped Concrete","hex":"#a0a09a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"stamped","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of stamp"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stamped"},{"term":"concrete","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Real, actual, tangible.","Analogous to the categories of algebraic objects which category theory was created to generalize, in the sense of having objects which can be thought of as sets equipped with some additional structure. Formally, equipped with a faithful functor to the category of sets."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A building material created by mixing cement, water, and aggregate such as gravel and sand.","Such a material whose cement is Portland cement or a similar limestone derivative."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/concrete"}],"entries":[{"title":"Stamped concrete","blocks":[{"text":"Stamped concrete is concrete that has been imprinted, or that is patterned, textured, or embossed to resemble brick, slate, flagstone, stone, tile, wood, or various other patterns and textures. The practice of stamping concrete for various purposes began with the ancient Romans. In the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, concrete was sometimes stamped with contractor names and years during public works projects, but by the late twentieth century the term \"stamped concrete\" came to refer primarily to decorative concrete produced with special modern techniques for use in patios, sidewalks, driveways, pool decks, and interior flooring."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Stampedconcrete1.jpg/330px-Stampedconcrete1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stamped_concrete","wikidata":"Q7597466"},{"title":"Concrete","blocks":[{"text":"Concrete is a composite material composed of aggregate bound together with a fluid cement that cures to a solid. It is the second-most-used substance, the most widely used building material, and the most-manufactured material in the world. Cement-bound concrete differs from the less rigid, less durable asphaltic concrete, which has a bituminous binder."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Bloczek_betonowy.jpg/330px-Bloczek_betonowy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concrete","wikidata":"Q22657"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Pantheon_%28Roma%29_-_Front.jpg/500px-Pantheon_%28Roma%29_-_Front.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Pantheon_%28Roma%29_-_Front.jpg/1280px-Pantheon_%28Roma%29_-_Front.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Pantheon_%28Roma%29_-_Front.jpg/500px-Pantheon_%28Roma%29_-_Front.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ancient stamped concrete of the Pantheon in Rome","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APantheon_(Roma)_-_Front.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Sebastian_County_Road_4G_Bridge%2C_Stamped_in_the_Concrete.JPG/500px-Sebastian_County_Road_4G_Bridge%2C_Stamped_in_the_Concrete.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Sebastian_County_Road_4G_Bridge%2C_Stamped_in_the_Concrete.JPG/1280px-Sebastian_County_Road_4G_Bridge%2C_Stamped_in_the_Concrete.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Sebastian_County_Road_4G_Bridge%2C_Stamped_in_the_Concrete.JPG/500px-Sebastian_County_Road_4G_Bridge%2C_Stamped_in_the_Concret